    "case_id": "653",
    "case_summary": "A 45-year-old woman presented with a 4-day history of left-sided pyelonephritis. Medical history included 9 spontaneous abortions and one completed pregnancy, with previous workup negative for antiphospholipid syndrome and protein C deficiency. No personal or family history of clotting or liver disease. Patient was a lifetime nonsmoker, seldom drank alcohol, and was not taking any medications or supplements.\n\nPatient was febrile at triage but hemodynamically stable. Physical examination revealed diaphoresis and left flank pain. No right-upper-quadrant tenderness or hepatomegaly was noted.\n\n- Laboratory findings: Initially elevated international normalized ratio of 1.4 (resolved to 1.0 within 3 days), activated prothrombin time 29.3s (normal range 28.0-38.0s). Other hepatic function tests and standard investigations were unremarkable.- Imaging Studies:  1. Abdominal CT scan:     - Cortical enhancement of left kidney with surrounding inflammatory stranding     - Multifocal peripheral dilation of bile ducts at right liver lobe     - Relative atrophy of left lobe     - Liver showed smooth contours with no signs of cirrhosis    2. MRI:     Image Title: Arterial-phase fat-saturated T1-weighted magnetic resonance image     Image Description: Demonstrates a 2.8-cm filling defect in the common hepatic artery",
